:: rohit_morning_d3
<<img "pg_room_kavya_pov.png" "the PG room, 6:30 AM">>
Rohit is a geological formation under blankets, the chainsaw snore cycling through its gears. You press a key on the laptop   the screen wakes, VS Code, a mechanical keyboard on USB, dual monitors. One tear escapes. Just one; Arjun's body dispenses tears like a bureaucracy processes refunds.

<div class="dialogue">"Bhai, itni subah?"<span class="gloss-en">Bro, this early?</span></div>

One eye, open.

"Morning walk." Arjun's voice, Arjun's phrasing. You practised this sentence fourteen times on the ride.

Both eyes now. He stares at you like a man told the sun rises in the west.

<div class="dialogue">"Tum? Morning walk? Bhai, tu bimaar hai kya?"<span class="gloss-en">You? A morning walk? Bro, are you sick?</span></div>

"Neend nahi aayi."<span class="gloss-en">Couldn't sleep.</span> You shrug   practised, Arjun-lazy, one shoulder up and down with the casual indifference of a boy who considers effort a philosophical position.

Rohit squints for three seconds. Then collapses. <div class="dialogue">"Pagal ho raha hai tu."<span class="gloss-en">You're losing it.</span></div> Thirty seconds later: snoring.

First test: passed. The body did the acting   the height, the voice, the shrug. Your mind just had to not ruin it.
<<run setup.flag("rohit_pg_passed")>>\
<<run setup.stat("masc_comfort", 3, "kavya")>>
<<hubreturn>>


:: pg_chai_circle_d3
The PG landing: four plastic chairs, a gas stove, the ₹3 chai operation of engineering students who've decided Raju's ₹15 is an extravagance.

Amit, second-year IT, spectacles: <div class="dialogue">"Oye Arjun, chai peega?"<span class="gloss-en">Hey Arjun, want some tea?</span></div>

You take the steel glass. The chai tastes like a chemistry experiment that achieved sentience and begged for mercy. You drink it.

<div class="dialogue">"Bhai, kitne baje aaya subah? 4:45 pe meri alarm baji   tera Pulsar ki awaaz aayi."<span class="gloss-en">Bro, what time did you get in? My alarm went at 4:45   I heard your Pulsar.</span></div>

"Morning walk." Arjun's casual disregard for explanation.

You stand with them. Legs apart, never crossed. One hand in the left jeans pocket, Arjun's habit. The easy half-lean against the wall. Weight on one foot, hip cocked   //no.// Not hip-cocked. That's yours. You straighten. Both feet. Wider stance.

Nobody questions it. You're invisible in the way Arjun is invisible. No one monitors your clothes, your posture, how you hold your cup. This is what it feels like to not be watched, and it's so intoxicating you almost forget to drink the terrible chai.
<<run setup.flag("pg_chai_done_d3")>>\
<<run setup.discover("not being watched", "kavya")>>\
<<run setup.stat("masc_comfort", 2, "kavya")>>
<<hubreturn>>


:: amit_laptop_d3
<div class="dialogue">"Arjun bhai, ek problem hai."<span class="gloss-en">Arjun bhai, there's a problem.</span></div>

Amit adjusts his spectacles. <div class="dialogue">"Mera laptop... pop-ups. Ads on browser. Speed gone. Virus lagta hai."<span class="gloss-en">My laptop... pop-ups. Ads on the browser. Speed's gone. Looks like a virus.</span></div>

"Dikha."<span class="gloss-en">Show me.</span> One word. Arjun's economy of speech for tech.

The virus is obvious to your trained eye   browser hijacker, probably from cracked software. Toolbar extensions injecting ads, a startup redirect, registry mods hiding the process from Task Manager.

Command Prompt. Navigate the system folders, kill the malicious executables, strip the registry entries, reset the browser. Twelve minutes.

<div class="dialogue">"Bhai. Tu normally itna technical nahi karta. Aaj kya hua?"<span class="gloss-en">Bro. You don't normally go this technical. What happened today?</span></div>

Shit. Arjun would've run a simple antivirus scan. You just did manual malware removal requiring command-line knowledge beyond his demonstrated level. Another brilliance leak.

"YouTube tutorial. Kal raat."<span class="gloss-en">YouTube tutorial. Last night.</span>

Amit accepts it. <div class="dialogue">"Tu genius hai. Biryani khila dunga."<span class="gloss-en">You're a genius. I'll buy you biryani.</span></div> The biryani is never coming, but the trust is banked   Amit will vouch for Arjun's presence in the PG.
<<run setup.flag("amit_laptop_fixed_d3")>>\
<<run setup.stat("k_coding", 2, "kavya")>>\
<<run setup.stat("rel_amit", 3, "kavya")>>
<<hubreturn>>


:: raju_d3
<<img "pg_room_kavya_pov.png" "Raju's tapri">>
Raju bhaiya's mustache at its morning apex. The cutting chai slides across the counter before you order   two years of Arjun's patronage encoded in the motion.

<div class="dialogue">"Arjun baba! Free chai ka third day. Enjoy kar."<span class="gloss-en">Arjun baba! Third day of free tea. Enjoy.</span></div>

The chai is perfect. Metallic steel-vessel taste, the depth of leaves boiled not steeped, the sweetness calibrated by fifteen years of practice.

"Raju bhaiya, aaj kuch naya?"<span class="gloss-en">Raju bhaiya, anything new today?</span>

<div class="dialogue">"Naya? Woh fruit wali Tai ka ladka aa raha hai Mumbai se. Bada engineer. Google mein kaam karta hai. Shayad tujhse mil sake. Networking, baba."<span class="gloss-en">New? The fruit-seller aunty's son is coming from Mumbai. Big engineer. Works at Google. Maybe he'll meet you. Networking, baba.</span></div>

A Google engineer. And you're being offered the intro while wearing the body of a CS student who doesn't know you exist. The irony is layered enough to qualify as a thesis.

"Sure. Milwa dena."<span class="gloss-en">Sure. Introduce us.</span>

<div class="dialogue">"Kal. Subah 8 baje. Yahan. Raju ka tapri   India ka naya LinkedIn."<span class="gloss-en">Tomorrow. 8 AM. Here. Raju's stall   India's new LinkedIn.</span></div>
<<run setup.flag("google_engineer_intro_d3")>>\
